# Example environment file for local secrets.
#
# Copy this file to `.env` and fill in the real values, then `source .env`.
# `.env` is gitignored (`.gitignore:34`) and must never be committed; this
# `.example` carries no value and is the shape, so it is safe to commit and it
# is the one name the ignore rule lets back in.
#
# Nothing in this repository reads these variables at build or test time. The
# compiler does not know they exist. They are here for two callers only: a
# `wrangler` run started by hand from this machine, and the documentation of
# what the deploy workflow expects to find in GitHub's secret store.

# ---- Cloudflare ----
# The site's deploy path is a Cloudflare Pages project that wrangler uploads to.
# The architecture and the whole procedure live in `site/README.md` § Deployment,
# which is their only home; what follows is only what a key needs said next to it.
#
# Use a CUSTOM API token, never the Global API Key: that one covers the whole
# account and cannot be revoked selectively, so losing it costs everything at
# once instead of one project.
#
# For a deploy that stops at `pages.dev`, ONE policy is enough:
#
#   Policy 1 - resource: Entire Account
#     Developer Platform -> Cloudflare Pages   Edit   create the project, deploy
#     Account & Billing  -> Account Settings   Read   read the account id
#
# The day heroes-lang.org moves onto Cloudflare a second policy is needed, and
# the trap is that the PERMISSION LIST ITSELF CHANGES with the resource selector:
# under "Entire Account" there is no Zone, no DNS and no Zone Settings entry at
# all, and the things it does offer under "DNS & Zones" (Account DNS Settings,
# DNS Firewall, DNS View, Registrar) are none of what is needed.
#
#   Policy 2 - resource: All Domains
#     Zone                                     Edit   create zones, read assigned NS
#     DNS                                      Edit   records
#     Zone Settings                            Edit   SSL/TLS, Always Use HTTPS
#
# All Domains rather than Specified Domains: `Zone: Edit` across every domain of
# the account is what grants `zone.create`, and at the start the zone does not
# exist to be selected.
#
# Left out on purpose: Email Routing, which can reroute the domain's mail, and
# Account Rulesets. Cloudflare Web Analytics has no token permission at all.
#
# Give the token a short expiry. A token that outlives the work it was made for
# is a key left in a door, and the expiry is the one protection that does not
# depend on anybody remembering.
#
# One trap worth an hour: the current builder issues ACCOUNT-OWNED tokens, the
# ones with a `cfat_` prefix, and `/user/tokens/verify` answers `Invalid API
# Token` for every one of them, because that endpoint is user-scoped and these
# tokens belong to the account. Verify at
# `/accounts/<account_id>/tokens/verify` instead. Every other endpoint takes the
# token as it is.
#
# Same variable name the deploy workflow expects as a repository secret, so that
# the local invocation and the CI one cannot drift apart.
export CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN=your-cloudflare-api-token-here

# The account that owns the Pages project. wrangler infers it from the token
# when the token reaches exactly one account; the workflow and any direct API
# call want it stated. It is an identifier rather than a credential, and it is
# still only written here, because a file that is half secret is a file people
# stop reading carefully.
export CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID=your-cloudflare-account-id-here
